  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...\nActive weather dominates the long term as we watch a strong \nsystem moving into the panhandle over the weekend. this front is\nexpected to reach the northeastern gulf coast Friday evening and\ncontinue inland overnight into Saturday where it will steadily\nshift southeastward through Sunday morning. This system is still \nexpected to bring more significant precipitation amounts through \nthe weekend with weak AR influence, though totals have decreased\nthrough the more recent model runs. Still looking at much of the\npanhandle seeing 1 to 2 inches on Saturday, with closer to 0.5 to\n1.5 inches on Sunday with the highest amounts in the southern\npanhandle. Orographic enhancement is expected in the Fairweather,\nChilkat, and Coast Ranges, likely increasing totals in inland \nareas such as Juneau, Petersburg, and Ketchikan. With the longer \nduration of this event, some locations may approach 3 inches over\nthe course of the weekend.\n\nAdditionally, as the front pushes inland Saturday, winds between \nCape Suckling and Cape Fairweather will strengthen to 25 to 30 mph,\nwhere a weak barrier jet forms as the low moves further east. \nIncreasing through Friday afternoon and evening, southerly winds \nin Lynn Canal are expected to reach 25 to 30 mph through Friday \nevening with the possibility of sustained winds reaching 35 mph \nas the front makes landfall through early Saturday morning. The \nrest of the channels should see south to southeasterly 20 to 25\nmph winds increase through Saturday and decrease again into \nSunday. Certain inner channel hot spots, such as Frederick Sound \ninto Stephens Passage and around Point Couverden, will see the \nhigher end of this range, with periods of 25 to 30 mph winds \npossible. As the low continues to traverse inland Saturday \nafternoon, Clarence Strait should feel a period of these stronger \nsoutherly winds increase as well.\n\nShowers will continue behind the front Sunday, keeping the \npanhandle relatively wet. Another front with decent agreement is \nexpected to reach the panhandle Monday, though uncertainty remains\nthe in the position of the low. Leaning towards the EC runs with \nkeeping the low in the northern gulf Monday before it travels \nsoutheastward along the coast Tuesday, which would allow for more \nactivity to continue Tuesday and into the extended range. \nTemperatures through this period will persist in the upper 50s and\n60s.",
